## 📝 Quick Reference

### JOIN Syntax

```sql
SELECT   columns
FROM     table1 t1
JOIN_TYPE table2 t2
    ON t1.key = t2.key
WHERE    condition
ORDER BY column;
```

### JOIN Types Cheat Sheet

| JOIN Type | Returns | NULLs | Use When |
|---|---|---|---|
| `INNER JOIN` | Matching rows only | No NULLs | Only want records in both tables |
| `LEFT JOIN` | All LEFT + matching RIGHT | Right side NULLs | Keep all records from main table |
| `RIGHT JOIN` | All RIGHT + matching LEFT | Left side NULLs | Keep all records from second table |
| `FULL OUTER` | Everything from both | Both sides NULLs | See all unmatched records |

---

### NULL Trick — Find Unmatched Records

```sql
-- Customers with NO orders
SELECT c.first_name
FROM   customers c
LEFT JOIN orders o ON c.customer_id = o.customer_id
WHERE  o.order_id IS NULL;
```

---

### MySQL FULL OUTER Workaround

```sql
SELECT columns FROM t1 LEFT JOIN t2  ON t1.id = t2.id
UNION
SELECT columns FROM t1 RIGHT JOIN t2 ON t1.id = t2.id;
```

---

## ⚠️ Common Mistakes

| Mistake | Problem | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Forgetting ON clause | MySQL does a CROSS JOIN | Always write `ON t1.key = t2.key` |
| Ambiguous column names | MySQL error on shared column names | Prefix with alias: `c.customer_id` |
| WHERE instead of ON | Changes LEFT JOIN behaviour | Join condition goes in ON, filters go in WHERE |
| UNION ALL for FULL OUTER | Duplicate matched rows | Use UNION not UNION ALL |

